<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#federalism">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Federalism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as federalisation, federal autonomy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Federalism is a system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and constituent units. Proposals to federalise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> were discussed as a way to satisfy its many nationalities but were never carried out. The term is also used to describe the substantial local autonomy that cities and provinces enjoyed within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/federalism.jpg" alt="Federalism" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_esp_Rep._Federal,_Rep._Unitaria_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
